WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:03.530
Hello, Neil and Buzz.

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:04.440
This certainly has to be

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.280
the most historic
telephone call ever made.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.040
For every American this has to be

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.783
the proudest day of our lives.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.770
If you go back to the
dawn of the space age,

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:20.800
they were very much the right
stuff, fighter pilot jocks,

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.066
the first generation were recruited out

00:00:23.066 --> 00:00:25.520
in the U.S. Air Force.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:28.160
The profile of someone
who would go into space

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:31.203
has a certain look and feel,
a certain prototype to it.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:35.580
I mean, really up until
now, if you had a disability

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:39.070
you would have naturally
assumed that spaceflight

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:40.523
was not an option for you.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:48.750
We believe it's time to
assess the feasibility

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.923
of sending astronauts with
physical disabilities into space.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:56.440
Without the force of gravity

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:58.820
and in a totally different environment

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:01.020
we have an opportunity
to create a new norm,

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.770
and an environment where, really,

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:05.890
we can accommodate any type of body

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:09.050
and there is no disabled
or able-bodied body,

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:10.503
rather everyone's the same.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.470
You look back in history
to seeing the difference

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:17.550
that putting women in space made,

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:20.660
and putting people of color in space made.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:23.130
The same, I'm sure will be true

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:27.173
when we open those doors to
people with disabilities.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.610
It's just the start, I think,

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.423
and I don't know where it's gonna lead.

00:01:42.102 --> 00:01:44.820
In 1959, the first U.S. astronauts

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:45.703
were selected.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:49.183
All seven men were military personnel,

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:53.557
but before these astronauts
came the Gallaudet Eleven.

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:59.190
The Gallaudet Eleven were
a real important group

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:03.643
and I don't know if you can
see this picture or not.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:07.620
The Gallaudet Project was really

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:10.340
initiated by Ashton Graybiel,

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:13.973
who was one of the
pioneers in space flight.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.850
He tested all of the early astronauts.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:21.490
He was especially interested
in human responses

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:25.523
to acceleration and to unusual forces.

00:02:27.999 --> 00:02:30.280
Dr. Graybiel, who was working with NASA,

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:31.710
chose 11 volunteers

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:34.750
who shared a very specific characteristic.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:36.640
They were all deaf.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.560
Spinal Meningitis had disrupted

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:40.480
their inner ear vestibular system

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:44.030
also known as the labyrinth,
which is devoted exclusively

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.133
to the detection of acceleration.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:50.000
For a decade they underwent
various experiments

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.570
that included living 12 days
in a 20 foot rotation room,

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:56.500
and sailing in extremely
rough waters during a storm

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:57.813
in the North Atlantic.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.250
In every single provocative situation

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:06.720
in which he tested the
Labyrinthine loss subjects

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.903
there were no symptoms of motion sickness.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:12.310
The funny part of it is throughout it

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:16.510
most of the crew and all
of the people coordinating

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:19.590
the labyrinthine loss subjects

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:21.860
were vomiting their hearts out.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:24.883
As people in the trade
say, they were barfing.

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:29.040
And the Labyrinthine loss subjects

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.963
were just sitting playing cards.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:32.753
Having a ball.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:35.820
NASA was then better able

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.800
to understand how the
body's sensory systems work

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:40.830
and the effects of
prolonged weightlessness

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:42.543
and space motion sickness.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:44.830
I think there's a lot of reasons

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:46.560
why people with disabilities

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.160
actually are really well
suited to be astronauts.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:50.760
The first thing that comes to mind

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:52.240
is that people with disabilities

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:54.200
are used to being very nimble

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:57.760
and to being very dynamic and responsive,

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:01.090
to thinking quickly and
problem solving, right?

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:02.950
And so all of those are characteristics

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:06.300
that would be highly desirable
for an astronaut, right?

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:07.900
Like having that quick response time

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:09.270
and being able to think creatively

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:12.233
about new solutions to
problems that might develop.

00:04:15.953 --> 00:04:17.020
While the Gallaudet Eleven

00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:19.130
made human spaceflight possible

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:21.470
they've been forgotten from its history.

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:23.880
And since then differently
abled individuals

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.680
have largely been cut out
of the astronaut process

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:28.723
until now.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.360
Earlier this year the
European Space Agency

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:34.070
announced that it would not only be hiring

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:37.450
new astronauts for the
first time since 2008,

00:04:37.450 --> 00:04:40.390
but it would also be running
a feasibility project

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:43.163
in the hopes of training its
first disabled astronaut.

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:45.920
This was something we'd
been talking about in house

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:47.710
for the past two or three years.

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:49.690
I used to call it Project X

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:52.280
with the Head of the Astronaut Center

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:56.000
because we kind of asked
ourselves the question

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.600
we've always had these basic
minimum medical standards,

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:01.790
but they've always called for somebody

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:05.050
that's able-bodied in
the traditional sense.

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:07.090
But the question was, well, why?

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:10.120
Have we asked ourselves
whether we can fly somebody

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:13.980
with a physical disability
to the space station?

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:18.080
And so alongside the call
for the mainstream core

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:21.210
astronaut call, we've introduced
this feasibility study

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:23.113
for a parastronaut.

00:05:24.307 --> 00:05:26.100
Like the Gallaudet Eleven Program

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:29.020
there are very specific
requirements for those taking part

00:05:29.020 --> 00:05:32.180
in the ESA's feasibility project.

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:33.480
In this first call,

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.790
they selected different types of amputees

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:39.350
at different levels of
lower limb involvement,

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:42.120
as well as individuals of short stature.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:44.420
And honestly, I think it
was a really smart move

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:49.420
because these are clearly very
well-defined disabilities,

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:52.870
but they are primarily
musculoskeletal in nature,

00:05:52.870 --> 00:05:55.240
fairly uncomplicated from
a physiologic standpoint

00:05:55.240 --> 00:05:57.340
yet there's this musculoskeletal feature

00:05:57.340 --> 00:05:58.850
that creates a visible difference

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:00.260
and therefore categorizes them

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:03.090
as disabled, so to speak.

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:05.450
And for that reason

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:09.130
when you think about all the
complexities of spaceflight,

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:10.590
of being an astronaut,

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:13.820
it's definitely like a
really good starting point.

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:16.100
The ESA has committed an initial budget

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:19.210
of 1 million Euros, and will
be working with two or three

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:22.140
selected applicants on
what technical adaptations

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:25.410
existing space crafts
and stations may need.

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:26.760
Ignition.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:28.580
For example, for the launch vehicle,

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:31.840
for the crew capsule that gets
them up to the space station

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:34.180
we, the Europeans, don't
have our own vehicles

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:35.490
so that means we're gonna have to work

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:37.470
with the U.S. providers.

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:39.080
There's Boeing and the SpaceX

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:41.320
who send astronauts up
to the space station

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:43.610
so we're gonna have to
work technically with them.

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:46.540
They're gonna have to also
train to be a regular astronaut

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:48.580
because what's important for us is this

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:50.800
is not kind of a joy ride.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:53.170
We want them to be able to participate

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:54.560
in a meaningful mission

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:57.310
aboard the International Space Station,

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:59.340
to undertake maintenance tasks,

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:01.663
to run scientific experiments and so on.

00:07:03.658 --> 00:07:04.690
You know, in the disability community

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:06.740
we use this phrase
called universal design,

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:08.850
and I think what the parastronaut program

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:10.890
is pushing us to think about is, again,

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:13.770
designing our environments so
that they're more universal

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:18.160
and suited for everybody and
every type of ability level

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:20.010
from day one without having to retrofit it

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:21.150
later down the road.

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:23.590
So, I think that thinking
about universal design

00:07:23.590 --> 00:07:27.070
as it relates to
settlements in other planets

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:30.370
or space in general helps
us to really reframe that

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:33.430
and to think about
whether you're an infant,

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:35.460
or someone who's 100 years old,

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:37.130
whether you're large or small,

00:07:37.130 --> 00:07:40.620
whether you have a
visible disability or not,

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:44.100
what type of environment can we envision

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:46.470
that reduces the barriers for everyone

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:48.070
to the greatest extent possible?

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:51.170
Some of the adaptive support systems

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:53.580
these individuals use on a daily basis

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:56.220
may no longer be needed in space.

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:58.450
An environment that could
level the playing field

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:01.133
for many who are considered
disabled on Earth.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:05.150
When you look at the
International Space Station

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:07.000
everybody floats up there.

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:10.780
Your use of your feet in
space isn't really needed.

00:08:10.780 --> 00:08:14.780
And in fact, astronauts come
back with very soft feet

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:18.010
because they haven't been
used for all that time.

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:21.790
So, the adaptations on the space station,

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:22.730
they will be considered,

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:27.730
but I imagined them to be
not massively challenging

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:30.937
because of the environment
that you are in.

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:36.130
Once you're in zero G environment

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:38.600
the legs are not tremendously useful.

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:40.890
Of course the astronauts
spend a lot of time

00:08:40.890 --> 00:08:44.480
doing physical exercise
to maintain their health

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:46.590
so that when they come back to Earth

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:47.560
they're in a good state

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:49.980
because if you don't do
so you lose muscle mass

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:51.673
and you also lose bone mass.

00:08:53.150 --> 00:08:54.310
Astronauts can lose

00:08:54.310 --> 00:08:58.083
as much as 10% of bone density
during six months in space.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:02.460
To offset the effect they
exercise for two hours a day,

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:04.730
at least six days a week.

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:08.460
They do an hour of cardio,
perhaps running on a treadmill,

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:10.320
exercising on a bicycle.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:11.490
And they do an hour

00:09:11.490 --> 00:09:14.760
of what you and I might
refer to as weightlifters.

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:16.300
Now in space, you can't lift weights.

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:20.110
Nothing has a weight because
you don't feel gravity,

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:24.910
but we use hydraulic
Springs, like resistance,

00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:28.220
to tension those muscles and so on.

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:29.820
Maintaining bone and muscle mass

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:32.610
will be critical for parastronauts,

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:34.830
but as humans venture further from Earth

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:38.220
more room on rockets and space
stations is needed for fuel

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:39.963
so space is at a premium.

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:45.400
One innovation that could let exercise

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:46.920
take up less space and time

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:51.323
is the high frequency impulse
for microgravity, or HIFIm.

00:09:52.413 --> 00:09:55.730
HIFIm promises to reduce the
astronauts current workouts

00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:58.840
to just six minutes a day on one machine.

00:09:58.840 --> 00:10:02.373
It does this by enabling
astronauts to jump in zero gravity.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:08.650
So, to be able to jump is a fantastic way

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:10.710
to load the skeletal system,

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:14.300
build up muscle density
and also cardiovascularly

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:16.033
it's ferocious on the body.

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:19.010
Through ESA bedrest studies

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:23.160
we've proven that jumping
will mitigate bone density

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:27.140
and muscle mass loss
with just 50 jumps a day.

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:29.240
That's four to six minutes a day.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:30.360
That's amazing.

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:32.820
The time that's gonna give astronauts back

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:36.230
to do experiments and
more science in space.

00:10:36.230 --> 00:10:39.610
But you can't just jump
because you've got to make sure

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:42.740
the load and vibration does
not go through the spacecraft.

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:47.680
So HIFIm is designed to absorb
its own load and vibration

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:49.180
when an individual is jumping.

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:51.920
HIFIm has been developed

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:55.250
with both astronauts and
parastronauts in mind.

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:57.010
Former Royal Marine, Lee Spencer,

00:10:57.010 --> 00:10:58.290
has been trialing the machine

00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:00.690
throughout its prototype phase.

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:03.160
My left leg was really badly damaged

00:11:03.160 --> 00:11:05.680
when I lost my right leg,

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:09.580
and that really means that I
can't actually sprint or jump,

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:13.520
but the great thing that
this does is it allows you,

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:16.000
as an amputee, to train in a way

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:17.800
that I've not been able to train before,

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:20.430
and that's with explosive muscle movements

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:22.640
where you can jump again.

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:24.260
We can actually control the load

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:28.300
that's going through his
prosthetic and then into his limb.

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:31.890
So we can develop the bone
density and muscle mass

00:11:31.890 --> 00:11:34.260
through that limb, through
both limbs, through his pelvis,

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:35.170
through his spine,

00:11:35.170 --> 00:11:40.170
but also condition the points
where the stump of his leg

00:11:40.770 --> 00:11:44.300
meets the prosthetic and we
can condition and develop that

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:46.160
and make it stronger and more use

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:48.363
to getting that load pass through it.

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:50.670
Although it's not yet part

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:52.610
of the feasibility project,

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:56.050
the UK Space Agency has been
supporting the development

00:11:56.050 --> 00:11:58.170
of the HIFIm.

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:01.460
Later this year it will
be tested in micro gravity

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:04.220
on a parabolic flight
where Lee will become

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:07.713
the first disabled person
to jump in zero gravity.

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:11.010
We, in society,

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:15.120
tend to define someone
by their disability.

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:17.350
I thought I had to redefine who I was

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:18.587
within the parameters of disability,

00:12:18.587 --> 00:12:20.700
and I was wrong to do that.

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:24.400
And in no other walk of life
is someone defined by something

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:27.000
they're not good at, or
something they can't do,

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:28.830
but if you have a disability

00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:32.950
then that can tend to define who you are

00:12:32.950 --> 00:12:35.150
certainly from other
people's point of view.

00:12:36.079 --> 00:12:37.960
You know, if you look at
the definition of disability

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:39.760
by the World Health Organization,

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:42.280
where we've really moved
globally is a understanding

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:44.150
that disability is a combination

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:47.580
of both an individual's impairment,

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:50.210
but then also how that
interacts with the environment.

00:12:50.210 --> 00:12:52.840
And so you can have a specific impairment,

00:12:52.840 --> 00:12:54.910
take the amputee for example,

00:12:54.910 --> 00:12:58.210
but if that individual is
then working and living

00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:01.540
within an environment
that is fully accessible

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:04.390
then the disability, so to
speak, really melts away, right?

00:13:04.390 --> 00:13:07.440
And so disability is really
defined by our environment.

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:10.480
And, you know, that's why all of this work

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:13.120
around the parastronauts and
thinking about adapting space

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:14.730
is really so innovative and exciting

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:16.670
'cause I think we have a opportunity

00:13:16.670 --> 00:13:20.990
to really totally redefine
what is a, so to speak,

00:13:20.990 --> 00:13:23.453
normal body and what needs to be adapted.

00:13:25.510 --> 00:13:28.160
If the Feasibility Project is successful

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:30.990
the European Space Agency
will be looking to include

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:33.370
parastronauts in future missions,

00:13:33.370 --> 00:13:37.403
including a trip to the
International Space Station by 2025.

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:40.860
But beyond making space more accessible

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:44.010
the ESA is also hoping a
diverse crew will give them

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:47.363
an even greater chance of
success in future missions.

00:13:48.630 --> 00:13:52.150
We know that diverse
teams are better teams.

00:13:52.150 --> 00:13:53.260
We know that people

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:55.890
that don't all have the
same life experience,

00:13:55.890 --> 00:13:58.460
and they don't have all
thinking the same way,

00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:01.610
come from different countries
work better together.

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:04.360
It's the fantastic thing about
working European Space Agency

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:07.540
with 22 different
countries working together

00:14:07.540 --> 00:14:09.930
and those different cultural backgrounds

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:13.210
and ways of thinking make us strong.

00:14:13.210 --> 00:14:15.290
And the same is true if you've got a crew

00:14:15.290 --> 00:14:17.290
trying to go to Mars for the first time,

00:14:17.290 --> 00:14:20.610
you want a mix of people,
different genders,

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:22.580
different backgrounds, different countries

00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:23.780
to make a stronger team.

00:14:27.470 --> 00:14:28.590
The space industry

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:30.590
is known for its innovation

00:14:30.590 --> 00:14:33.800
and the research and findings
from the Feasibility Program

00:14:33.800 --> 00:14:36.567
might not only produce
the first parastronaut,

00:14:36.567 --> 00:14:40.300
but also have an impact on
how disability and adaptation

00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:42.650
are treated back here on Earth.

00:14:42.650 --> 00:14:43.890
A lot of the adaptive technology

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:45.620
that we use here in the U.S.

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:48.310
you know, the speed of
research and development,

00:14:48.310 --> 00:14:49.750
the speed of innovation

00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:51.550
sometimes can be a little bit stagnant.

00:14:51.550 --> 00:14:54.470
And so I think that reframing
that and thinking about,

00:14:54.470 --> 00:14:57.480
well, how do you best empower
people with disabilities

00:14:57.480 --> 00:14:58.943
in a space environment.

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:02.150
How do you best empower
people to be astronauts

00:15:02.150 --> 00:15:03.360
has the ability to translate

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:06.383
to really push us a lot more
quickly in our innovation here.

